{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg --files collab/yang-clojure | rg '(1794524600000|1794422563000-architect-d1-profile-amendment-r2|continuation-4|1794700000000|r1[456])' | xargs cat\"","aggregated_output":"# D1 r9b Jupiter: corpus rebuild started, incomplete\n\nThis updates `1794698000000-engineer-d1-r9-jupiter.gpt-6.1-terra.findings.md` by reference. **Partial only: 8 of 155 fixtures passed the static gate; 147 remain not run.** No Jupiter bounds declaration, tagged run, D1 corpus record, verification result, or companion has been completed. No commit; the retention-location owner decision remains pending.\n\nThe batch pass outcome now retains the six actual segment addresses returned by persistence: input, snapshot, evidence, profile, root and stamped-root. The batch record refuses a pass missing any of these addresses. A storage delta of zero is permitted because deduplicated replay can add no bytes. The record remains bookkeeping, not a verification result. Each admission is generated by the existing fresh run and recorded with its origin; no historical coordinates are invented.\n\nScratch progress is `target/d1-r9b-outcomes.edn` (SHA-256 at this stopping point `cfffefb46cebc49c74a44b6db739d32bb5e8bd631d3b382d9d9b6faf773f1f3b`), with its store at `target/d1-r9b-store/content.jing` (26,292,420 bytes). These are local scratch, not published retention. The first process was interrupted after seven recorded passes; a fresh process reopened the store and progress file and added one pass. A separate fresh process then reopened the store, fetched all 48 addresses for the eight passes, and checked that each input and snapshot origin equalled the recorded admission origin. This demonstrates process-interruption resume for this sample, not a power-loss test or the completed corpus.\n\nRed first: an outcome missing addresses was accepted before the change and refused afterward; a zero-byte pass was refused before the change and accepted afterward. The focused batch test passes 1 test / 9 assertions. Before the zero-byte adjustment the standing battery passed 57 tests / 702 assertions; the focused test covers the subsequent adjustment. The restarted corpus invocation passed 1 test / 9 assertions. clj-kondo reports 0 errors and 0 warnings on the three changed files. The frozen `slice9_site_tables.edn` SHA-256 remains `e435ac4072ba0a3c278fbe91918d22184b43f0a8d6c1e397c09f68dd629beb75`. No full oracle suite or tagged lane was run.\n\nTo resume, keep both scratch paths together, set `target/d1-corpus-run.edn` to their paths and the desired `:D1_CORPUS_BATCH`, then run `clojure -M:test -n yang.latte-oracle-test -v yang.latte-oracle-test/test-d1-corpus-static-gates` with the configured JVM environment. Outcomes are final, and the progress file identifies the remaining fixtures. After the full rebuild, measure actual Jupiter lane, heap, size and phase maxima before declaring ceilings; do not borrow laptop bounds. The r9 limitations on verifier completeness and provenance remain unresolved and must be resolved before any verification-result claim.\n\n## Continuation on 2026-11-16\n\nThe existing run resumed with a 25-fixture batch, using the same outcomes file and store, with spot checks enabled. The invocation completed successfully (1 test, 9 assertions, 0 failures/errors); all 25 new outcomes passed. A separate process reopened the store and fetched all six retained addresses for each of the 33 passes, and checked that each fetched input and snapshot origin matched its recorded admission origin. Its summary was **155 fixtures: 33 pass, 0 refused, 122 not run; 198 addresses checked**. This is still a partial static corpus rebuild, not a verification result.\n\nAt this stopping point `target/d1-r9b-outcomes.edn` SHA-256 is `ea2b6829d6a33a5bfe32e312dc6b93e91e4181e633b9c46de45471e2227264a8`; `target/d1-r9b-store/content.jing` is 154,053,237 bytes. The batch log is `target/d1-r9b-batch-2.log` and the reopen check is `target/d1-r9b-check-2.log`. The check was run using `java -cp \"$(clojure -Spath -A:test)\" clojure.main target/d1-r9b-check.clj`; two preliminary attempts accidentally invoked the test alias's broad default suite and were interrupted, so they are not completed test results. The frozen table SHA-256 remains `e435ac4072ba0a3c278fbe91918d22184b43f0a8d6c1e397c09f68dd629beb75`.\n\nNo code was changed in this continuation. No Jupiter bounds declaration, tagged lane, corpus correspondence record, verification result, or companion has been completed. No commit was made. Preserve both scratch paths together; the configured next batch remains 25 fixtures. The previous red-first and kondo results above describe the preexisting changes, not new tests from this continuation. The r9 verifier and provenance limitations remain open.\n\n## Continuation: third batch on 2026-11-16\n\nThe next 25 fixtures passed the static gates (1 test, 9 assertions, 0 failures/errors; `target/d1-r9b-batch-3.log`). A fresh process reopened the shared store, fetched all six retained addresses for each pass, and checked that the input and snapshot origins matched the recorded admissions (`target/d1-r9b-check-3.log`): **155 fixtures, 58 pass, 0 refused, 97 not run; 348 addresses checked**. This remains a partial static rebuild, not a verification result. The progress file SHA-256 at that checkpoint was `802386972c1e8fbd128511e9be18a3bfe6d2593aa6a97e143f02e8d9c46b9b48`; the store was 461,259,401 bytes. The frozen table SHA-256 was still `e435ac4072ba0a3c278fbe91918d22184b43f0a8d6c1e397c09f68dd629beb75`. No code changed in this continuation and no commit was made. The undeclared bounds, verifier limitations, and missing tagged run and publications described above remain open.\n\nThe fourth batch then completed with 25 more passes (1 test, 9 assertions, 0 failures/errors; `target/d1-r9b-batch-4.log`), reaching 83 pass and 72 not run. At this checkpoint the outcomes SHA-256 was `89be38012ead5dcd16d9ca6c9316c4c01f0d7665fb0759e817d791b166138c02` and the store was 560,561,698 bytes. The independent reopen check above covers the preceding 58 passes; it has not yet been repeated for this checkpoint.\n\nThe fifth batch completed with 25 more passes (1 test, 9 assertions, 0 failures/errors; `target/d1-r9b-batch-5.log`): 108 pass and 47 not run. The outcomes SHA-256 at that checkpoint was `b2b69880cf0a3567ed022be644f50d4bc2c892323d107103baf4b89719028ead`; store size was 628,794,487 bytes. The separate reopen check still covers the earlier 58-pass checkpoint only.\n\nThe sixth batch completed with 25 further passes (1 test, 9 assertions, 0 failures/errors; `target/d1-r9b-batch-6.log`), reaching 133 pass and 22 not run. The checkpoint outcomes SHA-256 was `71eb3504c89b43404cdb3e33d06ee4f9b4f8286fafa84acfd47b5cc42530dac0`; store size was 707,662,785 bytes. The independent reopen check has not yet covered this checkpoint.\n\n## Completed Jupiter static corpus rebuild, 2026-11-16\n\nThe final batch completed the 155-fixture static rebuild (1 test, 9 assertions, 0 failures/errors; `target/d1-r9b-batch-7.log`). The progress record has **155 pass, 0 refused, 0 not run**. Every pass records its fresh admission origin and six retained addresses, including input and snapshot. A separate fresh process reopened the final store, fetched all **930** recorded addresses, and checked each input and snapshot origin against its recorded admission (`target/d1-r9b-check-final.log`). This verifies retrieval and origin equality for those objects, not admission authenticity, trace completeness, or durable published retention.\n\nKeep `target/d1-r9b-outcomes.edn` (SHA-256 `bd0e7e113a2e10a80f26402443c2f18be7cc2701e7261077b7e0772c55acda39`) together with `target/d1-r9b-store/content.jing` (733,758,551 bytes). These are scratch, not an owner-approved retention location. The frozen v1 table hash remains `e435ac4072ba0a3c278fbe91918d22184b43f0a8d6c1e397c09f68dd629beb75`. The outcomes show maximum per-fixture store growth 17,887,854 bytes, elapsed 112,615 ms, sites 451, and row closure 12,193; site total 20,805. These are static observations, not declared ceilings; store growth includes shared deduplication and elapsed includes differing batch conditions.\n\n**Remaining:** no Jupiter bounds declaration is finalized. Heap sampling was not durably recorded for these batches; C-lane quantities, serialized trace sizes, per-phase times and baseline/added work are not measured. No ceiling-governed tagged run, verification-result publication, or companion corpus record exists. The verifier and provenance gaps documented in r9 remain open. Consequently this is not a verification or reliance claim. No code changes were made during this continuation; prior red-first and kondo results are as recorded above. No commit was made. Next: retain these exact scratch artifacts, measure the missing quantities on Jupiter, implement and red-test the missing bounded/verifier gates, declare finite ceilings before the tagged run, and record any exceedance as a failed run rather than raising a ceiling.\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-11-17 09:39:07 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-11-17 04:39:07 EST\n\nThe requested publication is **incomplete**. I recorded the worktree findings in [the r14 findings file](/home/sto/workspace/datomworld-d1/collab/yang-clojure/1794790000000-engineer-d1-r14-companion-subset-retention.gpt-6.1-terra.findings.md).\n\nThe subset extraction was interrupted after producing a partial store; I removed that incomplete artifact. The companion and verification result remain blocked by missing recipe, provenance, image-verification, and complete two-lane trace evidence. The full-store retention owner remains pending designation.\n\nThe standing battery passed **58 tests and 708 assertions**. Kondo reported **0 errors and 1 warning** across the inherited battery. The frozen v1 file’s SHA-256 remains unchanged. No commit was made.Completed-GMT: 2026-11-17 09:45:07 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-11-17 16:45:07 +07\n\nDelivered a partial prerequisite for the executable recipe: [correspondence_dependency.clj](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-d1/src/cljc/yang/correspondence_dependency.clj) stores and retrieves byte-preserving, content-addressed dependency bundles. It does **not** establish or execute the compiler dependency closure.\n\nThe requested items 1–2 checkpoint remains incomplete. The named r14 findings and architect rulings, along with the Jupiter retained store described in the handoff, are absent from this checkout. The separate laptop store was not substituted for them.\n\nFocused tests passed: 9 tests, 76 assertions. Kondo reported zero errors and warnings, and the frozen v1 table hash remains unchanged. No full oracle suite ran and nothing was committed.\n\nThe worktree findings and exact remaining work are recorded in [r15 findings](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-d1/collab/yang-clojure/1794795000000-engineer-d1-r15-upstream-construction.gpt-6.1-terra.findings.md).# D1 r16 — retained-input inventory; closure remains open\n\nWorktree mirror; no commit. This is a partial investigation, not a recipe, publication, verification result, or reliance claim. Preserve `target/d1-r9e-retained-store` and its outcomes together. The requested r14 findings, two named ruling files, and Jupiter r9b store are absent here, as already recorded in r15. This laptop's r9e corpus is a separate run.\n\n## What the capture actually retains\n\nThe r9e capture implementation in `test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj`, `kernel-compile`, records the actual fixture, forms, call, assembled program, options, EDN representations of the latter four, and the source admission coordinate. The six addressed roles written by `d1-persist-and-check-fixture!` are evidence, input, text profile, binding snapshot, pre-stamp root and stamped root. The snapshot carries bindings, C1 rows and context constructors. The stored profile is `yang.correspondence-text/profile`: JVM and serialization/reader details, not an executable compiler profile. The producer replay calls the checkout's `yang/compile-program`, then checks the resulting root; equality under that checkout does not establish which compiler bytes were used originally or provide a fetchable compiler and transitive runtime.\n\nThe capture path does not include a compiler byte bundle, grammar revision, independently recorded expansion environment or an expansion-event log. The program and options may include expanded library forms and actual options; they are valuable inputs but do not establish the missing producer history or prove a complete dependency closure. This observation does not assert that this particular compilation necessarily invoked the row-native expander. In particular, its events cannot be invented from a resulting tree. `yin.vm.macro/expand-batch` has explicit event/attempt records when that expander is used, while `expand` returns only the tree packet. Any claim of no relevant expansion attempts also needs evidence, not inference from absent records.\n\nThe r9e findings name `ccd3648dc4e1877dfed0e507d6f6c0971115bfc2` and report a clean start; later r9g measurements used uncommitted helpers. The commit exists; the current HEAD is `e4ac21142397d27bf6627121c29ddfb57a602970`. The historical and current tracked versions of `yang/clojure.cljc`, `yin/vm/macro.cljc`, `test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj`, and `deps.edn` compare equal, and these files are currently clean. This is useful candidate-source evidence, not proof of the complete original runtime or all historical uncommitted bytes, and the commit is not an addressed retrievable dependency bundle. The r15 byte bundle implementation stores supplied bytes and explicitly does not prove completeness.\n\n## Consequences and next work\n\nNo compiler closure was bundled or attached to a recipe: doing so on the available evidence would silently assert the missing provenance. First recover the original executable/classpath and actual expansion context or event records, including evidence of any inapplicable stages. Establish the real transitive dependency inventory and verified replay/rebind semantics, then use the r15 store for the identified bytes and reference it from the profile addressed by the recipe. If those original facts cannot be recovered, a newly captured compilation under explicit recorded coordinates and environment is a distinct admission, not a retroactive history of r9e.\n\nThe C1 rows in the snapshot are not by themselves cc3's published, verified metadata and instruction-provenance side records. No image derivation checks or complete per-fixture trace evidence were generated this round. The legacy `run-guest-machine` still discards its counter; `yang.correspondence-lane/run-measured!` separately reports aggregate steps and `read-trace!` checks the drain, but prior measurement records are not retained complete trace vectors or a bounded verification result. The row-lane-only restriction, unfinished bounds declaration, and retention-owner decision remain.\n\nNo full oracle suite was run. Frozen v1 evidence SHA-256: `e435ac4072ba0a3c278fbe91918d22184b43f0a8d6c1e397c09f68dd629beb75`. No production or test code changed in this round.\n\n## Direct retained-object sample\n\nA fresh JVM reopened the r9e store and fetched the `:minimal-last` input, snapshot and profile from the addresses in the r9e outcomes. Input keys were `:admission-origin`, `:call`, `:call-edn`, `:fixture`, `:forms`, `:forms-edn`, `:options`, `:options-edn`, `:program`, and `:program-edn`; snapshot keys were `:bindings`, `:constructors`, `:fixture`, `:metadata`, `:origin`, `:root`, and `:stamped-root`. Input and snapshot origins agreed: `[:source :program [\"d1-r8-corpus-313b026f-bd01-4362-b885-e12f9fa6f757\" 0] 0]`. The fetched profile is the described text profile, recording Clojure 1.12.4 and Java 21.0.2. Its address is `:segment/blake3-ab1263e1c5b304bda2d1875f4fa78a7a91ca82d4c9435dffa262288e7a9863b0`; the input is `:segment/blake3-ac696f04cf12957ff0276c8f91c242a1bde65d3a602ccbfa1177733a731c28f4` and the snapshot is `:segment/blake3-755b6da0b4b4aec3457b0ac340a3f3b08db002e42c1812017b70cd011a94e78f`. This is one direct sample, not a new full-corpus traversal or proof that no other independently stored content exists. The outcomes file SHA-256 is `1eb58dd86c1410585e563636b5405bb1f2b66e8b41be9a58ff528a77ed6569ce`.\n{\"type\":\"thread.started\",\"thread_id\":\"01a1ad9a-1b29-70a3-be6b-f7b26721a674\"}\n{\"type\":\"turn.started\"}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_0\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"I’ll review the signed ruling, the verification contract, and the implementation and evidence at the specified commit. I’ll keep this static and use the quoted remote test results as given.\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_1\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'git status --short --branch && git rev-parse HEAD && cat docs/design/datom.world.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_1\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'git status --short --branch && git rev-parse HEAD && cat docs/design/datom.world.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"git: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-unwyjvTw' (errno=Operation not permitted)\\n2026-11-06 04:25:31.137 xcodebuild[26969:111646384]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\\n2026-11-06 04:25:31.415 xcodebuild[26969:111646383] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \\\"Input/output error\\\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\\ngit: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-ix1VNfGo' (errno=Operation not permitted)\\n2026-11-06 04:25:32.009 xcodebuild[26973:111646405]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\\n2026-11-06 04:25:32.188 xcodebuild[26973:111646404] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \\\"Input/output error\\\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\\n## attribution-as-query\\n?? BRIEF-aaq.md\\n?? probe/aaq_probe.clj\\n?? probe/clojure_test_final.txt\\n?? probe/focused_run.txt\\n?? probe/focused_run2.txt\\n?? probe/oracle_committed_tree.txt\\n?? probe/oracle_final.txt\\n?? probe/oracle_run1.txt\\n?? probe/oracle_run2.txt\\n?? probe/out10.txt\\n?? probe/out11.txt\\n?? probe/out12.txt\\n?? probe/out13.txt\\n?? probe/out14.txt\\n?? probe/out15.txt\\n?? probe/out16.txt\\n?? probe/out17.txt\\n?? probe/out18.txt\\n?? probe/out19.txt\\n?? probe/out20.txt\\n?? probe/out21.txt\\n?? probe/out22.txt\\n?? probe/out23.txt\\n?? probe/out24.txt\\n?? probe/out25.txt\\n?? probe/out9.txt\\n?? probe/regen_run.txt\\n?? probe/regen_run2.txt\\ngit: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-Dav0rLuc' (errno=Operation not permitted)\\n2026-11-06 04:25:33.411 xcodebuild[26981:111646458]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\\n2026-11-06 04:25:33.589 xcodebuild[26981:111646457] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \\\"Input/output error\\\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\\ngit: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-TdFUsbAh' (errno=Operation not permitted)\\n2026-11-06 04:25:34.114 xcodebuild[26985:111646473]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\\n2026-11-06 04:25:34.292 xcodebuild[26985:111646471] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \\\"Input/output error\\\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\\nd794382e5035597c0cbd399e786b99cb68f04e99\\n---\\ndescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\\n---\\n\\n# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\\n\\n## Core Philosophy\\n\\nEverything is data.\\nInterpretation > abstraction.\\nExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\\nRestrictions are a feature.\\n\\n### Foundational Axioms\\n\\n1. **Everything is a Stream**: Portable I/O and system data flow through append-only streams; hosts may also install explicit local functions that perform direct I/O.\\n2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\\n   At system scale, a \\\"system\\\" is an emergent property of interpreters of\\n   pure data, not an object, service, or stored artifact. Find the\\n   interpreters and the data they share to find the system.\\n3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\\n4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\\n\\n## Non-Negotiable Invariants\\n\\n- Do not introduce hidden global state.\\n- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\\n- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\\n- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\\n- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\\n- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\\n- Do not lose information (unitarity): streams are append-only and no datom is destroyed, only reinterpreted. The Universal AST is never lost: it is always retrievable wherever code is lowered, by address or by a query over its datoms. Every derived form (semantic vector, native images, indexes) is an interpretation of it and may drop facts only because the AST remains.\\n\\n## Tuples and Datoms\\n\\nTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\\nA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\\nSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\\n\\nSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\\n\\n## Streams\\n\\n`dao.stream` is an I/O monad and the system's portable I/O ABI. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\\nHosts may install arbitrary local functions, including direct I/O; their availability and migration semantics must be explicit.\\nPortable behavior cannot assume them; portable side effects appear as stream emissions. Direct-I/O host modules are explicitly local and non-migratable (owner decision, 2026-10-23).\\nStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\\nStreams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\\n\\nA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\\nof interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\\nsemantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\\nstream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\\nthe very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\\nanswers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\\nexists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\\nthe other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\\nand the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\\nbecomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\\nthe stream's own.\\n\\nFunctions can take anything and return anything.\\nAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate portably with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Portable agent behavior is specified through stream effects; host-local functions may also perform direct I/O.\\n\\nSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\\nSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\\n\\n### Host Boundaries\\n\\nA host boundary is the dao.stream abstraction boundary; implementations do not add contracts.\\n[datom.world.abi.md](./datom.world.abi.md) owns the doctrine, owner rulings, taxonomy, floor and custody rules.\\n\\n## Architectural Commitments\\n\\n- **`dao.jing` is syntax, agents are semantics**: the content-addressed encoding stays passive and payload-agnostic.\\n- **Macros are stream topology**: an evaluator knows nothing about macro expansion; expansion happens on the syntax side of a medium boundary, upstream of any evaluator.\\n- **Peer observers, one stream**: a generic evaluator and `dao.space` independently observe the same stream topic, as described under Streams. This is not a claim about any two consumers regardless of what they consume.\\n- **Observers are independent and optional**: nothing requires a medium to have any particular set of consumers. Which observers exist for a stream is a per-composition choice, not a design-time commitment.\\n\\n## Design Principles\\n\\nCode quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?\\nStigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\\n\\n**Systems emerge from interpreters of pure data.** Streams are the\\ncomposition medium; each interpreter gives the same data a perspective\\nwithout collapsing interpretation into execution or owning the truth.\\n\\n- `dao.space` emerges from `dao.space.index` and `dao.space.query` over\\n  ordinary streams.\\n- ShiBi capability, metering, and budget emerge from different\\n  interpreters over the same tuples; no token object is stored.\\n- The four VMs interpret one Universal AST; many syntaxes feed that AST.\\n- The semantic AST in `dao.space` is queryable facts projected by the\\n  auto-index observer.\\n- Client and server are roles in one stigmergic behavior implemented by\\n  interpreters, not distinct kinds of nodes.\\n\\n**Derive, don't persist.** Before adding a tag, slot, or field to any\\ncanonical content-hashed structure (an AST grammar, a datom schema, a row\\nshape), check whether a correctly scoped Datalog query over\\n`dao.space.query/q` can already compute the fact. If it can, it belongs as a\\nquery or projection: new persisted structure migrates every downstream\\nconsumer and duplicates authority over a fact a query derives fresh. The\\nretired `:global` tag in\\n[`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md) is the\\ncase study, including the scoping bug that a naive query introduced.\\n\\nSee [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\\nSee [`docs/agents/vocabulary.md`](../agents/vocabulary.md) for domain vocabulary reference (biology, economics, physics, philosophy).\\n\\n## Code Style\\n\\n- Prefer simple data over rich types.\\n- Prefer total functions over partial ones.\\n- Prefer declarative pipelines over imperative logic.\\n- Avoid cleverness.\\n- Name things after what they do to streams.\\n\\n## Development Workflow\\n\\n- Do not invent abstractions that hide streams.\\n- Do not suggest mainstream frameworks unless explicitly asked.\\n- Do not optimize prematurely.\\n- When uncertain, ask at the architectural level, not the implementation level.\\n- Explanations should align with Plan 9 / Datomic / Lisp lineage.\\n- Program store writes go through `engine/store-put`; any other store write\\n  the store-write audit (`test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj`)\\n  detects must be on its allowlist; its docstring states the residual it\\n  leaves to review.\\n\\n### Problem Decomposition\\n\\nIf a problem appears complex:\\n1. Decompose it into streams.\\n2. Identify invariants.\\n3. Restrict degrees of freedom.\\n4. Make causality explicit.\\n5. Let structure emerge from constraints.\\n\\n## Operational & Specialized Guides\\n\\n- **Advanced Concepts**: See [`docs/agents/advanced-concepts.md`](../agents/advanced-concepts.md) for parallel transport, capability tokens, and entanglement.\\n- **Build & Test**: See [`docs/agents/build-n-test.md`](../agents/build-n-test.md) for build, lint, and test commands and TDD guidelines.\\n- **Engineering Team Roster**: See [`docs/agents/team.md`](../agents/team.md) for engineering team roles and LLM model assignments.\\n- **Agent Delegation**: See [`docs/agents/roles/orchestrator.md`](../agents/roles/orchestrator.md) for collaboration protocols and delegating tasks or reviews through `agy`, `cmd`, `glm`, `deepseek`, and `codex`; each orchestrator session has its own append-only log at `orchestrator-logs/<unix-time-stamp>-<brief-orchestrator-descriptive-name>.md` under the repository root.\\n- **Website & Content**: See [`docs/agents/format.md`](../agents/format.md) for `.chp` and `.blog` EDN/Hiccup file specifications.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"error\",\"message\":\"You’ve hit your usage limit. Upgrade to Pro (https://chatgpt.com/explore/pro), visit https://chatgpt.com/settings/usage to purchase more credits or try again at 5:01 AM.\"}\n{\"type\":\"turn.failed\",\"error\":{\"message\":\"You’ve hit your usage limit. Upgrade to Pro (https://chatgpt.com/explore/pro), visit https://chatgpt.com/settings/usage to purchase more credits or try again at 5:01 AM.\"}}\nDISPATCH_RC=1\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
