{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_38","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat collab/yang-clojure/1794422563000-engineer-d1-correspondence-r{2,3,4,5,6,7}.gpt-6.1-terra.findings.md collab/yang-clojure/1794422563000-engineer-d1-correspondence-r8.glm-5.3.findings.md collab/yang-clojure/1794700000000-engineer-d1-r9b-corpus-rebuild.gpt-6.1-terra.findings.md > /tmp/d1-r18-history.txt; wc -l /tmp/d1-r18-history.txt; rg -n 'RULING|invariant|bounds|origin|fidelity|frozen' /tmp/d1-r18-history.txt | tail -30\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_38","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat collab/yang-clojure/1794422563000-engineer-d1-correspondence-r{2,3,4,5,6,7}.gpt-6.1-terra.findings.md collab/yang-clojure/1794422563000-engineer-d1-correspondence-r8.glm-5.3.findings.md collab/yang-clojure/1794700000000-engineer-d1-r9b-corpus-rebuild.gpt-6.1-terra.findings.md > /tmp/d1-r18-history.txt; wc -l /tmp/d1-r18-history.txt; rg -n 'RULING|invariant|bounds|origin|fidelity|frozen' /tmp/d1-r18-history.txt | tail -30\"","aggregated_output":"     219 /tmp/d1-r18-history.txt\n68:This is a limitation of the stipulated initial plain `pr-str`/EDN profile for this admitted input, not a demonstrated change to the original compiler result and not a reason to weaken equality or invent an origin. The r3 root-only pilot did not test metadata/numeric fidelity and remains a feasibility observation only. Its old successful replay test was replaced by the explicit real-fixture refusal regression; it is not represented as an r4 passing gate. An architect/owner decision is needed on a replay-preserving representation/profile or on an explicitly revised admissible-input/fidelity rule; no such change was assumed here.\n74:The frozen v1 oracle table remains SHA-256 `e435ac4072ba0a3c278fbe91918d22184b43f0a8d6c1e397c09f68dd629beb75`. The row-lane-only restriction remains. No existing image or manifest contract is changed.\n78:Red-first: new profile namespace was initially missing; adding capture requirements first failed because `forms`, `call` and options lacked text captures; attempting the real fixture under the strict capture then exposed the numeric metadata fidelity failure. The failure was retained as a refusal regression, not converted to a successful reconstruction. The correspondence, text-profile, and Clojure call-site battery passed 49 tests / 638 assertions. The real-fixture refusal test passed 1 test / 5 assertions and the earlier static table/binding test passed 1 test / 12 assertions. clj-kondo 2026.08.04 reported 0 errors and 0 warnings across all touched code/test files. The full oracle suite and all tagged lanes were not run.\n79:# D1 continuation r5: amended fidelity and static replay pilot\n85:`yang.correspondence-text/profile` now includes `:fidelity-rule :fixed-jvm-integer-wrappers-v1`, so its content address differs from the r4 class-strict profile. Its numeric comparison permits equal Byte/Short/Integer/Long values via exact `long` comparison; all other admitted numeric leaves require same class and `.equals` (including Double NaN, signed zero and BigDecimal scale). Numeric dispatch occurs before generic `=`. Tests cover all four wrapper pairs, changed numeric coordinates, missing metadata, Long/BigInt, decimal scale, signed zero, NaN, collection kind and map/set iteration order. This is scoped to this experimental JVM profile and does not establish behavior for arbitrary programs.\n87:The map mismatch path includes the map entry's actual key; capture failures name fixture, supplied admission scope, ordinal and input. The real-fixture test identifies r4's original mismatch precisely: `:beta-eq-false` forms `[15 2]` metadata entry 2 is `:line`, whose value is Integer before printing and Long after reading, with equal value. The amended capture **was observed to pass** on this fixture; it retains the supplied fresh admission coordinate and all four text captures. It does not change the historical r4 failure.\n91:For the one `:beta-eq-false` admission, the new test captures the original input, reads the captured text, recompiles, and compares its root, oracle canonical-source-hash, producer-derived site table, C1 metadata, site binding, stamped root/metadata and explicit stamped-to-unstamped correspondence with the original compilation. It validates both row trees and their application coverage; stamping adds two unmarked applications per wrapper, accounted separately from the C1 markers per the post-port cc3 test. It stores input, profile, expectations, and both canonical row closures in a file-backed store, closes and reopens it, checks all retained row bodies, and repeats the comparisons using fetched input/expectations. This runs only static compilation: no tagged machine, trace or verifier result. Test store is deleted after the test and is not durable publication of a corpus record.\n93:Crucial limit: `d1-check-reconstruction!` **shares** `kernel-stamp-sites`, `cc3-oracle-binding` and the oracle source-hash function with the producer. Thus its site table is not an independently re-derived declaration and the check is not an independent verifier or completed r5 gate. The exact executable dependency/expansion closure and runtime/profile compatibility are not pinned; no cross-host claim is made. A per-fixture replay test passes only that fixture and does not establish the entire corpus. The frozen v1 evidence remains unchanged.\n97:TDD: the amended wrapper/NaN and profile-id tests first failed against the old class-strict implementation; the first replay test failed for missing helper; an attempted stamped-tree coverage assertion then failed because it incorrectly expected wrapper helpers to be marked, and was corrected to the post-port accounting. The diagnostic tests check the original `:line` key and retained scope/ordinal. The amended capture test passed 1 test / 7 assertions; the scoped capture-refusal test passed 1 test / 2 assertions; and the full pre/post-stamp reopened-store replay pilot passed 1 test / 6 assertions. The correspondence, text-profile and Clojure call-site battery passed 49 tests / 659 assertions. clj-kondo 2026.08.04 reported 0 errors and 0 warnings on the touched code/test files. Full oracle suite and tagged lanes were not run.\n99:Still required: independently derive the site declarations from compiled argument expressions; retain and pin the executable profile/interpreter/dependency and expansion closure; provide complete stage-specific refusal coverage and actual per-compilation gates for the corpus; retain the real record and recipe, companion and verification result under their proper ownership and bounds. The current successful pilot does not satisfy the r6 checkpoint of *all* gates complete. Row-lane-only remains the D1 restriction.\n106:New namespace `yang.correspondence-verifier` independently interprets post-port stamped AST argument expressions and their lexical bindings to derive `:site`, `:callee`, `:arg-shapes`, `:def` and `:delegation?`; it does not invoke the oracle's stamping, classification or declaration helpers. The context constructor names are obtained from the supplied context forms. Its source-hash implementation separately applies the first-occurrence generated-symbol normalization and SHA-256 rule to supplied source inputs. Its independent paired-tree traversal translates the two inserted stamp applications and reconstructs site-to-persisted-occurrence bindings from the source tree and supplied admission origin; it does not call `cc3-oracle-binding` or `cc3-correspond`.\n110:A separate pure `attributed?` query takes a pre-scoped fixture table, bindings, origin/root and two lane records. Synthetic tests require both lanes to report complete, reject missing lane events, wrong ambient shape, self-delegation, missing bindings, mismatched admission and mismatched root. These tests do not establish that a real lane's data is complete or validate the provenance of supplied inputs; the query's own documentation explicitly disclaims reliance. No real two-lane trace or record was used in this round.\n116:The frozen v1 table remains SHA-256 `e435ac4072ba0a3c278fbe91918d22184b43f0a8d6c1e397c09f68dd629beb75`. D1 continues to claim only the row lane.\n131:The temporary-store harness compiles each fixture under a fresh recorded admission, checks the independent reconstructed bindings against cc3's producer binding, materializes the **whole** v1 evidence, captured inputs and profile, both row closures, C1 metadata and reconstructed bindings, then closes and reopens the store. From fetched inputs it recompiles and checks the retained root; from fetched row closures and the frozen fixture's declarations/hash it runs the independent static gate. It mutates a declaration for the fixture and requires a `:site-declarations` refusal. This stores no second durable oracle table: it reads the fixture declaration from the fetched complete evidence map.\n145:The complete versioned recipe with executable compiler/profile/dependency and expansion-attempt closure is still not pinned or retained. The required instruction-provenance side records and image derivation chains for these particular admitted oracle trees have not been produced or independently verified; the landed linker publisher is not, by itself, proof that its publication origin and this harness's `[:source :program ...]` admission are interchangeable. Fixture-to-admission scoping, the aggregate record's exact representation, artifact inventory and durable discovery still need completion. The output of `check-fixture!` cannot stand in for these. Do not create a placeholder D1 record or manufacture origin/expansion information to fill the gaps. The row-lane-only restriction remains.\n156:`yang.correspondence-batch` is the resumable outcome record: `initialize!` refuses to reset an existing run; `record!` validates each outcome per status (a pass carries its admission origin and measured storage; a refusal names its stage), refuses unknown fixtures and malformed entries, and makes every recorded outcome final; writes go through a same-directory temporary file with fsync and an atomic move so a reader never sees a partial record. The corpus driver `d1-corpus-batch!` (oracle test namespace) runs the next N-or-fewer not-yet-recorded fixtures — each under a fresh recorded admission of the batch run, each through the full r7 per-fixture static gate — recording outcomes atomically; the deftest `test-d1-corpus-static-gates` drives it from a run-config file (scratch mode without one: 3 fixtures, resumability, finality, spot checks — the standing-battery form).\n164:All 155 fixtures ran through the full static gate (independent source hash + site declarations + binding reconstruction + C1 marker coverage) under fresh recorded admissions, each with its per-fixture declaration mutation refused at `:site-declarations`. The honest per-fixture enumeration in `1794422563000-engineer-d1-correspondence-r8.outcomes.edn` (SHA-256 `7c2f9a6a17bdd144ba67be0326dc4b6ebdb6a83a7297ab08ac82e4ef8aef943a`) reads **155 :pass, 0 :refused, 0 :not-run**; each pass entry records its admission origin, storage delta, elapsed, row counts, site count, and mutation-refusal stage. No fixture failed its gate, so there is nothing to attribute this round; the refusal machinery is instead evidenced by the mutation checks: every fixture refused a mutated declaration, and the five-class battery (:inputs, :source-hash, :site-declarations, :bindings, :metadata) refused at exactly its own stage on real fixture data (presence and all-true asserted in the scratch run; all-true asserted in every spot-enabled corpus batch).\n174:`collab/yang-clojure/1794671000001-d1-bounds-declaration-draft.md` drafts the D1 bounds declaration in the cc3 v2 format: pinned corpus/revision/host/JVM table, retained-object set, ceilings declared from the measured maxima with headroom (per-fixture storage 24 MB over 17.9 MB; aggregate 1 GB over 734.5 MB; row closure 20,000 over 12,193; sites 700 over 451; per-fixture elapsed 300 s over 136.5 s), the exact reproducible procedure, and the maximum-based pass/fail rule. Peak heap and all lane quantities are explicitly NOT declared — r9 must measure and declare them before its tagged run. DRAFT ONLY: no run has been performed under these ceilings.\n185:This updates `1794698000000-engineer-d1-r9-jupiter.gpt-6.1-terra.findings.md` by reference. **Partial only: 8 of 155 fixtures passed the static gate; 147 remain not run.** No Jupiter bounds declaration, tagged run, D1 corpus record, verification result, or companion has been completed. No commit; the retention-location owner decision remains pending.\n187:The batch pass outcome now retains the six actual segment addresses returned by persistence: input, snapshot, evidence, profile, root and stamped-root. The batch record refuses a pass missing any of these addresses. A storage delta of zero is permitted because deduplicated replay can add no bytes. The record remains bookkeeping, not a verification result. Each admission is generated by the existing fresh run and recorded with its origin; no historical coordinates are invented.\n189:Scratch progress is `target/d1-r9b-outcomes.edn` (SHA-256 at this stopping point `cfffefb46cebc49c74a44b6db739d32bb5e8bd631d3b382d9d9b6faf773f1f3b`), with its store at `target/d1-r9b-store/content.jing` (26,292,420 bytes). These are local scratch, not published retention. The first process was interrupted after seven recorded passes; a fresh process reopened the store and progress file and added one pass. A separate fresh process then reopened the store, fetched all 48 addresses for the eight passes, and checked that each input and snapshot origin equalled the recorded admission origin. This demonstrates process-interruption resume for this sample, not a power-loss test or the completed corpus.\n191:Red first: an outcome missing addresses was accepted before the change and refused afterward; a zero-byte pass was refused before the change and accepted afterward. The focused batch test passes 1 test / 9 assertions. Before the zero-byte adjustment the standing battery passed 57 tests / 702 assertions; the focused test covers the subsequent adjustment. The restarted corpus invocation passed 1 test / 9 assertions. clj-kondo reports 0 errors and 0 warnings on the three changed files. The frozen `slice9_site_tables.edn` SHA-256 remains `e435ac4072ba0a3c278fbe91918d22184b43f0a8d6c1e397c09f68dd629beb75`. No full oracle suite or tagged lane was run.\n193:To resume, keep both scratch paths together, set `target/d1-corpus-run.edn` to their paths and the desired `:D1_CORPUS_BATCH`, then run `clojure -M:test -n yang.latte-oracle-test -v yang.latte-oracle-test/test-d1-corpus-static-gates` with the configured JVM environment. Outcomes are final, and the progress file identifies the remaining fixtures. After the full rebuild, measure actual Jupiter lane, heap, size and phase maxima before declaring ceilings; do not borrow laptop bounds. The r9 limitations on verifier completeness and provenance remain unresolved and must be resolved before any verification-result claim.\n197:The existing run resumed with a 25-fixture batch, using the same outcomes file and store, with spot checks enabled. The invocation completed successfully (1 test, 9 assertions, 0 failures/errors); all 25 new outcomes passed. A separate process reopened the store and fetched all six retained addresses for each of the 33 passes, and checked that each fetched input and snapshot origin matched its recorded admission origin. Its summary was **155 fixtures: 33 pass, 0 refused, 122 not run; 198 addresses checked**. This is still a partial static corpus rebuild, not a verification result.\n199:At this stopping point `target/d1-r9b-outcomes.edn` SHA-256 is `ea2b6829d6a33a5bfe32e312dc6b93e91e4181e633b9c46de45471e2227264a8`; `target/d1-r9b-store/content.jing` is 154,053,237 bytes. The batch log is `target/d1-r9b-batch-2.log` and the reopen check is `target/d1-r9b-check-2.log`. The check was run using `java -cp \"$(clojure -Spath -A:test)\" clojure.main target/d1-r9b-check.clj`; two preliminary attempts accidentally invoked the test alias's broad default suite and were interrupted, so they are not completed test results. The frozen table SHA-256 remains `e435ac4072ba0a3c278fbe91918d22184b43f0a8d6c1e397c09f68dd629beb75`.\n201:No code was changed in this continuation. No Jupiter bounds declaration, tagged lane, corpus correspondence record, verification result, or companion has been completed. No commit was made. Preserve both scratch paths together; the configured next batch remains 25 fixtures. The previous red-first and kondo results above describe the preexisting changes, not new tests from this continuation. The r9 verifier and provenance limitations remain open.\n205:The next 25 fixtures passed the static gates (1 test, 9 assertions, 0 failures/errors; `target/d1-r9b-batch-3.log`). A fresh process reopened the shared store, fetched all six retained addresses for each pass, and checked that the input and snapshot origins matched the recorded admissions (`target/d1-r9b-check-3.log`): **155 fixtures, 58 pass, 0 refused, 97 not run; 348 addresses checked**. This remains a partial static rebuild, not a verification result. The progress file SHA-256 at that checkpoint was `802386972c1e8fbd128511e9be18a3bfe6d2593aa6a97e143f02e8d9c46b9b48`; the store was 461,259,401 bytes. The frozen table SHA-256 was still `e435ac4072ba0a3c278fbe91918d22184b43f0a8d6c1e397c09f68dd629beb75`. No code changed in this continuation and no commit was made. The undeclared bounds, verifier limitations, and missing tagged run and publications described above remain open.\n215:The final batch completed the 155-fixture static rebuild (1 test, 9 assertions, 0 failures/errors; `target/d1-r9b-batch-7.log`). The progress record has **155 pass, 0 refused, 0 not run**. Every pass records its fresh admission origin and six retained addresses, including input and snapshot. A separate fresh process reopened the final store, fetched all **930** recorded addresses, and checked each input and snapshot origin against its recorded admission (`target/d1-r9b-check-final.log`). This verifies retrieval and origin equality for those objects, not admission authenticity, trace completeness, or durable published retention.\n217:Keep `target/d1-r9b-outcomes.edn` (SHA-256 `bd0e7e113a2e10a80f26402443c2f18be7cc2701e7261077b7e0772c55acda39`) together with `target/d1-r9b-store/content.jing` (733,758,551 bytes). These are scratch, not an owner-approved retention location. The frozen v1 table hash remains `e435ac4072ba0a3c278fbe91918d22184b43f0a8d6c1e397c09f68dd629beb75`. The outcomes show maximum per-fixture store growth 17,887,854 bytes, elapsed 112,615 ms, sites 451, and row closure 12,193; site total 20,805. These are static observations, not declared ceilings; store growth includes shared deduplication and elapsed includes differing batch conditions.\n219:**Remaining:** no Jupiter bounds declaration is finalized. Heap sampling was not durably recorded for these batches; C-lane quantities, serialized trace sizes, per-phase times and baseline/added work are not measured. No ceiling-governed tagged run, verification-result publication, or companion corpus record exists. The verifier and provenance gaps documented in r9 remain open. Consequently this is not a verification or reliance claim. No code changes were made during this continuation; prior red-first and kondo results are as recorded above. No commit was made. Next: retain these exact scratch artifacts, measure the missing quantities on Jupiter, implement and red-test the missing bounded/verifier gates, declare finite ceilings before the tagged run, and record any exceedance as a failed run rather than raising a ceiling.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
